{
  "topic": "How lightning and thunder form",
  "language": "en",
  "category": "ciencia",
  "title": "What is lightning? ⚡",
  "hook": "What is lightning, really, and why does thunder rumble?",
  "presenter": "perro",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "role": "hook",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_hook",
      "narration": "What is lightning, really, and why does thunder rumble?",
      "on_screen_text": "What is lightning?",
      "highlight_words": [
        "lightning?"
      ],
      "sfx": "impact",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_cloud",
      "narration": "Inside a storm cloud, rising winds smash tiny ice crystals into soft hail, millions of times.",
      "on_screen_text": "Ice colliding in the cloud",
      "highlight_words": [
        "colliding"
      ],
      "sfx": "whoosh",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_charges",
      "narration": "Those collisions separate charges: positive on top, negative at the bottom, and the ground takes the opposite charge.",
      "on_screen_text": "Charges separate",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Charges"
      ],
      "sfx": "riser",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_leader",
      "narration": "When the difference gets huge, a channel of charged air comes down in steps, looking for the ground.",
      "on_screen_text": "It comes down in steps",
      "highlight_words": [
        "steps"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_strike",
      "narration": "A spark rises from the ground to meet it, and when they touch, the current rushes through: that's the flash.",
      "on_screen_text": "They touch and flash!",
      "highlight_words": [
        "flash!"
      ],
      "sfx": "impact",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_heat",
      "narration": "That channel heats the air to about thirty thousand degrees Celsius: five times hotter than the surface of the Sun.",
      "on_screen_text": "5× hotter than the Sun",
      "highlight_words": [
        "5×"
      ],
      "sfx": "riser",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_thunder",
      "narration": "The air expands so fast it bursts like an explosion, and that's thunder.",
      "on_screen_text": "That's thunder",
      "highlight_words": [
        "thunder"
      ],
      "sfx": "impact",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_count",
      "narration": "Since light arrives before sound, count the seconds: every three is roughly one kilometer.",
      "on_screen_text": "3 seconds ≈ 1 km",
      "highlight_words": [
        "3"
      ],
      "sfx": "pop",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "role": "surprise",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_tower",
      "narration": "And yes, lightning can strike the same place twice: the Empire State Building gets hit about twenty times a year.",
      "on_screen_text": "Twice, and more!",
      "highlight_words": [
        "more!"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "role": "closing",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ray_outro",
      "narration": "Send this to someone who's scared of thunder.",
      "on_screen_text": "Send this to a friend",
      "highlight_words": [
        "friend"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Inside a storm cloud, ice and soft hail collide and separate electric charges. A channel of charged air steps down, a spark rises from the ground, and when they meet you get the flash. Lightning heats the air to about 30,000 °C and its explosive expansion is thunder. Count the seconds: every 3 is roughly 1 km. Original 3D animation.",
